; 01-apply-an-implication-modus-ponens.kurt
;
; Apply an implication: modus ponens
;
; If the theory contains `A implies B` and `A`, write `B`. Kurt applies implication elimination
; automatically.

use A implies B  "A-to-B"
use A
B

use B implies C  "B-to-C"
C

; Kurt performs one inference step at a time. It derives `B` first and can then use that new fact
; to derive `C`.
;
; `A`, `B` and `C` are not declared: at its first use, Kurt declares each symbol itself, and prints
; what it inferred under the line, as the Kurt declarations it would take to write it out --
; `const A ; added constant` (one fixed thing) and `prop A 0 ; added statement places` (a
; statement, true or false). Lesson 20 says more about constants, variables and declarations.
